{
  "concept": "Because a point on the unit circle moves continuously with its angle, letting x approach c forces P(cos x, sin x) to approach Q(cos c, sin c), which geometrically establishes lim_{x->c} sin x = sin c and lim_{x->c} cos x = cos c, i.e. sine and cosine are continuous at every c.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "horizontal x-axis with rightward arrowhead and vertical y-axis with upward arrowhead, crossing at the origin",
    "unit circle centered at the origin, drawn in blue (solid on the right/visible portion, dashed on the far-left arc)",
    "fixed point Q on the circle at angle c, filled blue dot, labeled 'Q(cos c, sin c)'",
    "variable point P on the circle at angle x, filled red dot, labeled 'P(cos x, sin x)'",
    "radius segment from origin to Q drawn in dark/black",
    "radius segment from origin to P drawn in red",
    "shaded circular sector between the two radii (origin, P, Q) filled with a purple radial gradient",
    "angle arc from the positive x-axis to the Q-radius, labeled 'c'",
    "smaller angle arc from the positive x-axis to the P-radius, labeled 'x'",
    "curved arrow near Q indicating that P moves toward Q as x approaches c",
    "caption box at the bottom reading 'As x approaches c the point P approaches the point Q.'"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "angleX",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Angle x (point P)",
      "range": [0, 6.28, 0.01],
      "default": 0.4,
      "teaches": "Dragging x slides P along the circle and shrinks or grows the shaded sector; as x gets close to c the red radius, the sector, and the gap between P and Q all collapse toward zero, making the limit lim_{x->c} (cos x, sin x) = (cos c, sin c) visually obvious"
    },
    {
      "id": "angleC",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Target angle c (point Q)",
      "range": [0, 6.28, 0.01],
      "default": 1.0,
      "teaches": "Relocating Q to any angle c updates the radii, the sector, the angle arcs, and the coordinate labels together, showing that the same continuity argument holds at every point c on the circle, not just one special angle"
    },
    {
      "id": "approachAnim",
      "type": "animation",
      "label": "x -> c from both sides",
      "loop": true,
      "teaches": "The self-playing sweep drives x toward c first from below and then from above, so the learner sees P converge to Q from both directions — the two-sided limit behavior that underlies continuity"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Two angles, one circle",
      "narration": "A fixed angle c places Q(cos c, sin c) in blue and a variable angle x places P(cos x, sin x) in red; the purple sector between their radii measures the angular gap between them.",
      "state": { "angleC": 1.0, "angleX": 0.4 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Slide x toward c",
      "narration": "Drag the x slider up toward c: P travels along the circle toward Q, the red radius rotates onto the dark radius, and the shaded sector shrinks to nothing — the coordinates (cos x, sin x) are converging to (cos c, sin c).",
      "state": { "angleC": 1.0, "angleX": 0.95 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Move the target c",
      "narration": "Now drag the c slider to a new angle: Q, both radii, the sector, the angle arcs, and the labels all relocate together, confirming the limit-and-continuity argument works at any c, not just the one pictured.",
      "state": { "angleC": 2.4, "angleX": 2.35 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Approach from both sides",
      "narration": "Play the animation: x sweeps into c from below and then from above, so P closes on Q from both directions around the circle — exactly the two-sided limit lim_{x->c} that makes sine and cosine continuous everywhere.",
      "state": { "angleC": 1.0, "angleX": 1.0 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Continuity of sine and cosine is just the fact that the point (cos x, sin x) slides continuously around the unit circle as the angle x varies, so x -> c forces the coordinates to approach (cos c, sin c) from both sides.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Center the unit circle at the origin of a standard Cartesian axis pair with arrowheads. Draw the circle in medium blue, with the leftmost arc rendered dashed to echo the textbook's clipped view. Place Q as a solid blue dot in the upper region (angle c ~ 1 rad) with an italic serif label 'Q(cos c, sin c)' to its upper right, and P as a solid red dot at a smaller angle (x ~ 0.4 rad) with label 'P(cos x, sin x)' to its right. Connect origin-to-Q with a thin dark line and origin-to-P with a thin red line; fill the wedge between them with a soft purple radial gradient that fades outward. Add two concentric angle arcs at the origin: a larger one to the Q-radius labeled 'c' and a smaller one to the P-radius labeled 'x'. Put a small curved arrow just outside the circle near Q pointing from P's side toward Q. Below the figure, draw a light-gray bordered caption box containing 'As x approaches c the point P approaches the point Q.' Keep all angle-driven geometry (dot positions, radii, sector, arcs, labels) recomputed live from the x and c sliders so the figure stays consistent as they move."
}

